Step 1: Verify Git Installation
git --version

Expected:

git version 2.xx.x
Step 2: Configure Your Git Identity

Replace with your GitHub email and name.

git config --global user.name "Your Name"


Now configure your email:

git config --global user.email "your-email@example.com"

Step 3: Verify Configuration
git config --list

or

git config --global --list

Expected:

user.name=Chetana Gadbail
user.email=your-email@example.com
Step 4: Configure GitHub Authentication (Recommended)

GitHub no longer allows password authentication over HTTPS. The recommended approach is SSH keys.

Generate an SSH key
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "your-email@example.com"

Press Enter for all prompts to accept the defaults.

Example:

Enter file in which to save the key:
/home/ubuntu/.ssh/id_ed25519
Start the SSH agent
eval "$(ssh-agent -s)"
Add the SSH key
ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
Display the public key
cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub

You'll see something like:

ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAA... your-email@example.com

Copy the entire output.

Step 5: Add the SSH Key to GitHub
Sign in to GitHub.
Click your profile picture → Settings.
Select SSH and GPG keys.
Click New SSH key.
Give it a title (for example, k8s-master).
Paste the public key.
Click Add SSH key.
Step 6: Test the Connection
ssh -T git@github.com
